@meta
id: 07-19
title: Capstone — moving without arrow keys
chapter: 07
type: scenario
skills: goto-line, paragraph-motion, scroll-half, word-w, line-start
par: 120
minutes: 8
vscode: arrow keys, Ctrl+Home/End, Ctrl+G
open: order.provider.ts answers.txt
vimrc: set number

@brief
Arrow keys work in vim. They are also a trap: they keep your hand
off home row and they only ever move one character. Vim's real
motions move by word, line, paragraph, screen and file.

Two files are open. Read order.provider.ts, answer the five
questions in answers.txt, save both.

Rule of this level: no arrow keys, no mouse. The checker can tell.

Switch between the two open files with  :bn  (buffer next) - you
will learn that properly in chapter 16.

@keys
h j k l        left, down, up, right
w  b           forward / back one word      e = end of word
0  ^  $        start of line / first non-blank / end of line
gg  G          top of file / bottom of file
25G            jump to line 25       (or  :25  )
Ctrl-D Ctrl-U  half a screen down / up
{  }           previous / next blank line - moves by paragraph
Ctrl-G         say which line I am on
:set number    show line numbers down the side

@hints
G goes to the very last line. With :set number on, that answers
total_lines in one keystroke.
--
26G jumps straight to line 26. ^ then takes you to the first
non-blank character on it, so you can read the word.
--
To find CANCELLED without reading the file: type /CANCELLED and
Enter. Searching is a motion too - that is chapter 12.
--
:bn switches to the other open file. :wa writes all of them.

@solution
:set number
G             -> 37    total_lines
4G            -> import { exception ... }    last import
26G ^         -> if
30G ^         -> await
/CANCELLED    -> line 34

:bn           hop to answers.txt
... fill it in ...
:wa           write every open file

Habit to build: think in units. "three words forward" is 3w, not
fifteen presses of the right arrow.

